"Adult" if the age is 18 or over
"Under 18" otherwise

Display the result.

Part 9 - The Animal Class
Create a class called Animal.
The class should contain:

a private _name field of type string
a private _age field of type int
a public Name property
a public Age property
a constructor that sets the name and age


What is a field/property? Java Version? Why?
I will talk about this more in lectures but in java, for proper encapsulation, you typically did
private int age;

public void setAge(int age){
  this.age = age;
}

public int getAge(){
  return age;
}
In C#, this approach will work fine, but it is not typically how we provide access to fields. Instead, C# provides properties.
private int _age;

public int Age
{
    get { return _age; }
    set { _age = value; }
}
The property allows us to control how the value can be accessed or changed.
One advantage is that we do not have to explicitly call setAge and getAge. We can treat the property much like a variable:
dog.Age = 5;
Console.WriteLine(dog.Age)
The get code runs when we read the property, while the set code runs when we assign a value to it.
The special keyword value represents the value being assigned to the property.


Naming Convention
In this module we will use the following naming convention:

Private fields begin with _ and use lowercase/camelCase:

_name
_age
_breed

Public properties and methods begin with a capital letter:

Name
Age
Sound()

Part 11 - Sound() Method
In the Animal base class, create a method called Sound.
The Sound() method should display:
This animal makes a sound
In Java, you can override an inherited method simply by providing a new implementation in the derived class (with @Override used to indicate that this is intentional).
C# is a little different. A method must explicitly allow itself to be overridden. To do this, the base-class method is marked virtual:
public virtual void MakeSound()
{
    Console.WriteLine("Some sound");
}
A derived class can then provide its own implementation using override:
public override void MakeSound()
{
    Console.WriteLine("Woof!");
}
Think of it as:

Java asks for forgiveness; C# asks for permission. And it’s easier to ask for forgiveness.

So, in C#, virtual is essentially the base class saying:

“Derived classes are allowed to replace this method with their own implementation.”

If a method is not marked virtual (or abstract), a derived class cannot override it.

So now:
Override the Sound() method in Dog.
It should display:
Woof!
Taking into consideration what was written above.

Which Language?
Consider this familiar example:
char gradeEarned;

if (num &gt;= 70)
{
    gradeEarned = 'A';
}
else if (num &gt;= 60)
{
    gradeEarned = 'B';
}
else
{
    gradeEarned = 'C';
}

C# vs Java
Arrays are also very similar.
For example, suppose we have:
int[] listOfInt = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
The traditional for loop is also very similar.
Java:
for (int i = 0; i &lt; listOfInt.length; ++i)
{
    System.out.println(listOfInt[i]);
}
C#:
for (int i = 0; i &lt; listOfInt.Length; ++i)
{
    Console.WriteLine(listOfInt[i]);
}
Notice two small differences:

Java uses .length
C# uses .Length

Length is a property in C#.

The Ternary Conditional Operator
Sometimes we have a very simple if/else.
For example:
string result;

if (num &gt;= 50)
{
    result = "Pass";
}
else
{
    result = "Fail";
}
Most languages allows us to write this using the ternary conditional operator:
string result = num &gt;= 50 ? "Pass" : "Fail";
The general form is:
condition ? value_if_true : value_if_false
This is useful when the decision is simple.
Don’t use it to turn complicated logic into an unreadable mess.

String Formatting
We also have several ways of putting values into strings.
One familiar approach is concatenation:
string name = "Donny";
int age = 25;

Console.WriteLine("Name: " + name + ", Age: " + age);
But C# also provides string interpolation.
Console.WriteLine($"Name: {name}, Age: {age}");
The $ tells C# that the string contains interpolated expressions.

String Interpolation
Expressions can be placed directly inside { }.
string name = "Donny";
int age = 25;

Console.WriteLine($"Hello {name}");
Console.WriteLine($"You are {age} years old");
You can also put expressions inside.
Console.WriteLine($"Next year you will be {age + 1}");
This is likely to be much more convenient than constructing long strings using +.

Naming Conventions in C#
PascalCase
Use PascalCase for:

Classes
Methods
Properties
Public members

Each word starts with a capital letter.
public class Person
{
    public string Name { get; set; }

    public void IncreaseAge()
    {
    }
}
Examples:
Person
StudentAccount
CalculateAverage
FirstName

Access Modifiers: internal Classes
You already know public and private from Java.
C# adds a third one worth knowing: internal.
When you create a new project, some of the generated template code uses it on classes:
internal class Program
{
    // ...
}
An internal class is only visible within the same project.
Code in a different project can’t see or use it at all — not even to reference the class name.
public class Person   // visible to other projects too
{
}

internal class Helper // only visible inside this project
{
}

Namespaces
A namespace groups related classes together and helps avoid naming collisions.
namespace SchoolSystem
{
    public class Person
    {
    }
}
Two different libraries could each have their own Person class, as long as they live in different namespaces:
SchoolSystem.Person
HospitalSystem.Person
Using a namespace
Rather than writing the full name every time, we bring a namespace into scope with using:
using SchoolSystem;

Person person = new Person();
Can also do
namespace SchoolSystem;

public class Person{
}
Then the namespace applies to the whole file

Classes and Encapsulation
A class allows us to group related:

Data
Methods
Functionality

For example:
public class Person
{
    private string _name;
    private int _age;

    public void SayHello()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Hello!");
    }
}
The private fields are hidden from code outside the class.
This is encapsulation.
In a larger application, this allows a class to control how its data is accessed and changed.

Properties
In Java, you may have written:
private String name;

public void setName(String name)
{
    this.name = name;
}

public String getName()
{
    return name;
}
The methods control access to the private field.
This will work fine in C#, but it is not the usual way of doing things.
C# has a different mechanism for this: properties.

C# Properties
A property allows us to expose controlled access to data. A property will have a backing field that stores the data.
public class Person
{
    private string _name;

    public string Name
    {
        get
        {
            return _name;
        }

        set
        {
            _name = value;
        }
    }
}
A property has accessors.
The get accessor is used when reading the property.
The set accessor is used when assigning to it.
The private field _name is where the actual data is stored.

Using a Property
Instead of:
person.setName("Donny");
System.out.print(person.getName());
we can write:
person.Name = "Donny";

Console.Write(person.Name);
C# knows whether we are reading or writing the property.
person.Name = "Donny";  // set

Console.Write(person.Name);  // get
The property looks like a variable when we use it.

Auto-Implemented Properties
If we don’t need to write our own get and set logic, C# provides a shorthand.
public class Person
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
}
This is an auto-implemented property.
C# automatically provides the backing storage for us. The private field does still exist, we just don’t have a reference to it.
This is extremely common in C#.

Read-Only Properties
We don’t always want the outside world to be able to change a value.
For example:
public class Person
{
    public string Name { get; set; }

    public int Age { get; }
}
A property with only a get accessor cannot normally be assigned from outside the class.
The value can be assigned when the object is constructed:
public Person(int age)
{
    Age = age;
}
But it cannot subsequently be changed:
Person person = new Person(25);

person.Age = 26;
Think of this as:

“You can ask me what my age is, but you cannot change it.”

Private Setters
Sometimes we want the class itself to be able to change a value, but we don’t want other code to do so.
public class Person
{
    public int Age { get; private set; }

    public Person(int age)
    {
        Age = age;
    }

    public void IncreaseAge()
    {
        ++Age;
    }
}
Outside the class:
Person person = new Person(25);

Console.WriteLine(person.Age);  // ✅

person.Age = 30;                // ❌
But the class itself can change the property:
person.IncreaseAge();           // ✅

Properties vs Fields
It is important to distinguish between a field and a property.
private int _age;               // field

public int Age { get; set; }    // property
A field is a variable belonging to the object.
A property provides controlled access to data.
The property may use a field behind the scenes:
private int _age;

public int Age
{
    get
    {
        return _age;
    }

    set
    {
        _age = value;
    }
}
Or C# can generate the backing storage automatically:
public int Age { get; set; }

Why Properties Matter
Properties give us encapsulation.
We can start with:
public string Name { get; set; }
But later we might need validation:
private int _age;

public int Age
{
    get
    {
        return _age;
    }

    set
    {
        if (value &gt;= 0)
        {
            _age = value;
        }
    }
}
Here the property controls access to _age.
The setter can validate the value before changing the field.

Inheritance
C# uses : to specify inheritance.
Java:
public class Dog extends Animal
{
}
C#:
public class Dog : Animal
{
}
A derived class inherits members from its base class.

The base Class
Sometimes a derived class needs to access functionality from its base class.
C# uses the keyword:
base
(In Java you would’ve used super) For example:
public class Animal
{
    public string Name { get; set; }

    public void Speak()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Some animal noise");
    }
}
A derived class can call the base implementation:
public class Dog : Animal
{
    public void SpeakLikeADog()
    {
        base.Speak();
        Console.WriteLine("Woof!");
    }
}
base means: “the version belonging to my base class.”

Constructors and base
base can also be used when calling a base-class constructor.
public class Animal
{
    public Animal(string name)
    {
        Name = name;
    }

    public string Name { get; }
}
The derived class can call it:
public class Dog : Animal
{
    public Dog(string name)  : base(name)
    {

    }
}
The Dog constructor passes name to the Animal constructor.

Overriding Methods
Sometimes a derived class needs to provide its own version of a method.
In Java, you may remember:
@Override
public void Speak()
{
    System.out.println("Woof!");
}
In C#, you cannot override just any method.
The base class must give permission by marking the method as virtual.
public class Animal
{
    public virtual void Speak()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Some animal noise");
    }
}
The derived class can then use override:
public class Dog : Animal
{
    public override void Speak()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Woof!");
    }
}
Think of it as:

Java asks for forgiveness.
C# asks for permission.

virtual and override
The base class:
public virtual void Speak()
{
    Console.WriteLine("Some animal noise");
}
is saying:

“Derived classes are allowed to provide their own implementation of this method.”

The derived class:
public override void Speak()
{
    Console.WriteLine("Woof!");
}
is saying:

“I am providing that alternative implementation.”

base and override
An override doesn’t necessarily mean we have to completely throw away the base implementation.
We can call it using base.
public class Dog : Animal
{
    public override void Speak()
    {
        base.Speak();
        Console.WriteLine("...and then Woof!");
    }
}
So:
base.Speak();
means:
Call the implementation from my base class.

Putting It Together
A simple C# class might therefore look like:
public class Person
{
    private string _name;

    public string Name
    {
        get
        {
            return _name;
        }

        private set
        {
            _name = value;
        }
    }

    public int Age { get; private set; }

    public Person(string name, int age)
    {
        _name = name;
        Age = age;
    }

    public void IncreaseAge()
    {
        ++Age;
    }

    public void SayHello()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Hello, my name is {Name} and I am {Age}.");
    }
}

From Static UI to Dynamic Application
Last week we created our user interface using XAML.
For example:
&lt;VerticalStackLayout&gt;
    &lt;Label x:Name="lblAnswer" Text="Waiting..." /&gt;
    &lt;Button Text="Calculate" /&gt;
&lt;/VerticalStackLayout&gt;
This describes the initial structure of the page.
However, an application should not remain exactly as it was when it started.
While the application is running, we can change the UI using C#.
lblAnswer.Text = "The answer is 42";
We can also create new objects and add them to layouts.
Label newLabel = new Label
{
    Text = "Created while the app is running"
};

myLayout.Children.Add(newLabel);

Runtime Changes
For example, the following changes an existing Label:
lblAnswer.Text = "The conversion is: " + answer;
We can also change other properties:
lblAnswer.TextColor = Colors.Red;
lblAnswer.FontSize = 24;
Or create new controls:
Button button = new Button
{
    Text = "New Button"
};

myLayout.Children.Add(button);
If we can do something in XAML, we can often do the equivalent in C# at runtime.

Unsubscribing from an event
When we subscribe to an event using +=, we can also unsubscribe using -=.
If we subscribe as on the previous slide, we can later remove that subscription:
button.Clicked -= OnButtonClicked;






Warning


The method being added or removed must be the same method:
button.Clicked += OnButtonClicked;
button.Clicked -= OnButtonClicked;
Although -= doing an eventhandler that’s not already subscribed, won’t actually break anything.

Event Handlers
An event handler is a method that is called when an event occurs.
A typical handler looks like:
private void OnButtonClicked(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    // Code to run when the event occurs
}
The exact type of the event arguments depends on the event.
For a simple Button click, there is no additional information required, so we use:
EventArgs e
Other events can provide more information.

The sender Parameter
What is sender?
It is the object that raised the event.
For example, if a Button is clicked:
private void OnButtonClicked(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
}
sender refers to the Button that was clicked.
Although its declared type is object, the actual object can be a Button, Entry, Timer, or another object that raised the event.

Working with sender
If we know that the sender is a Button, we can access its Button properties.
private void OnButtonClicked(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    Button button = (Button)sender;

    button.Text = "Clicked!";
}
The cast:
(Button)sender
tells C#:

Treat this object as a Button.

One Handler, Multiple Buttons
The same event handler can be used by multiple controls.
&lt;Button Text="Red"
        Clicked="OnButtonClicked" /&gt;

&lt;Button Text="Blue"
        Clicked="OnButtonClicked" /&gt;
The handler can determine which Button raised the event.
private void OnButtonClicked(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    if (sender is Button button)
    {
        button.Text = "Clicked!";
    }
}
This is often safer than assuming that sender is always a particular type.

object and Pattern Matching
Remember that object is a general C# type.
An object can refer to an instance of many different classes.
We can check its actual type using pattern matching:
if (sender is Button button)
{
    button.Text = "Clicked!";
}
This means:

If sender is a Button, create a variable called button referring to it.

We can use the same idea with other controls.
if (sender is Entry entry)
{
    entry.Text = "Hello";
}

Event Arguments
The second parameter contains information about the event.
For a Button click:
EventArgs e
does not contain much additional information.
Other events provide more specialised information.
For example:
private void OnSwipe(object sender, SwipedEventArgs e)
{
    lblMessage.Text = $"Swiped {e.Direction}";
}
The SwipedEventArgs object tells us information about the swipe.

Creating Event Handlers in Visual Studio
Visual Studio can create an appropriate event handler for us.
In XAML, start typing:
Clicked=
Visual Studio will offer:
&lt;New Event Handler&gt;
Selecting this creates a suitable method in the code-behind.
For example:
private void Button_Clicked(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
}
This is useful because Visual Studio knows which event we are handling and can provide the appropriate event argument type.

The TextChanged Event
For example:
&lt;Entry x:Name="txtName"
       TextChanged="OnTextChanged" /&gt;
The handler can examine the new text:
private void OnTextChanged(object sender, TextChangedEventArgs e)
{
    lblMessage.Text = e.NewTextValue;
}
The event arguments contain useful information about what changed.
For example:
e.OldTextValue
e.NewTextValue

Checking for Empty Input
We can check whether an Entry contains text.
private void OnCalculateClicked(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(txtQuantity.Text))
    {
        lblResult.Text = "Please enter a quantity.";
        return;
    }

    // Continue with the calculation
}
return exits the method.
If the input is invalid, the rest of the calculation is not performed.

Checking for a Number
We can use TryParse to safely attempt a conversion.
if (double.TryParse(txtQuantity.Text, out double quantity))
{
    // quantity contains the number
}
else
{
    // The input was not a valid double
}
TryParse returns:

true if the conversion succeeds
false if the conversion fails

If it succeeds, the converted value is placed into quantity.

Handling Invalid Numbers
Putting this into our button event:
private void OnCalculateClicked(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    if (!double.TryParse(txtQuantity.Text, out double quantity))
    {
        lblResult.Text = "Please enter a valid number.";
        return;
    }

    lblResult.Text = $"Quantity entered: {quantity}";
}
The application does not crash when the user enters something such as:
hello
Instead, we handle the error ourselves.

Properties Can Protect Data
Validation does not always have to happen in the UI.
Suppose we have a class representing a person.
We might want to prevent an invalid age from being stored.
private int _age;

public int Age
{
    get
    {
        return _age;
    }
    set
    {
        if (value &gt;= 0 && value &lt;= 120)
        {
            _age = value;
        }
    }
}
The property controls how the field can be changed.

DispatcherTimer
There are different types of timers and I will come back to another type later.
For UI work in .NET MAUI, we can use the application’s dispatcher to create a timer.
private IDispatcherTimer timer;

private void StartTimer()
{
    timer = Dispatcher.CreateTimer();
    timer.Interval = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1);
    timer.Tick += OnTimerTick;
    timer.Start();
}
The timer will raise its Tick event every second.

Starting and Stopping a Timer
A timer can be started and stopped.
timer.Start();
and:
timer.Stop();
For example:
private void StartTimer()
{
    timer = Dispatcher.CreateTimer();
    timer.Interval = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1);
    timer.Tick += OnTimerTick;
    timer.Start();
}

private void StopTimer()
{
    timer?.Stop();
}
The ?. means that Stop() is only called if timer is not null.

Anonymous Functions
We have written a separate method for every event:
timer.Tick += OnTimerTick;
with:
private void OnTimerTick(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    lblTime.Text = DateTime.Now.ToLongTimeString();
}
But sometimes an event handler is very small and is only used once.
C# allows us to write the function directly where we subscribe to the event.
This is an anonymous function.

Lambda Expressions
In C#, anonymous functions are commonly written using lambda expressions.
A basic lambda looks like:
(parameters) =&gt; expression
or:
(parameters) =&gt;
{
    // statements
};
For example:
button.Clicked += (sender, e) =&gt;
{
    lblMessage.Text = "Button clicked!";
};
There is no separate method called OnButtonClicked.

Lambda Expression Example
Instead of:
timer.Tick += OnTimerTick;

private void OnTimerTick(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    lblTime.Text = DateTime.Now.ToLongTimeString();
}
we could write:
timer.Tick += (sender, e) =&gt;
{
    lblTime.Text = DateTime.Now.ToLongTimeString();
};
The event still works in exactly the same way.
The only difference is where the event-handling code is written.

Handling a Tap
The event handler can be written like any other event handler.
private void OnImageTapped(object sender, TappedEventArgs e)
{
    lblMessage.Text = "Image tapped!";
}
Notice that this event uses:
TappedEventArgs
rather than the generic:
EventArgs
The specialised event arguments can contain additional information about the gesture.

Adding a Gesture at Runtime
We can also create the gesture recogniser using C#.
TapGestureRecognizer tapGestureRecognizer = new TapGestureRecognizer();

tapGestureRecognizer.Tapped += OnImageTapped;

img1.GestureRecognizers.Add(tapGestureRecognizer);
This is another example of the relationship between XAML and runtime C#.
The XAML version defines the initial UI.
The C# version can create and attach the same type of functionality while the application is running.

TappedEventArgs e
The e parameter contains information about what happened.
Typing e. in Visual Studio and waiting for IntelliSense will show the properties and methods available.

e.Buttons can tell us which mouse button was used
e.GetPosition(...) can tell us where the tap occurred

void OnTapGestureRecognizerTapped(object sender, TappedEventArgs e)
{
    // Position inside the window
    Point? windowPosition = e.GetPosition(null);

    // Position relative to an Image
    Point? relativeToImagePosition = e.GetPosition(image);

    // Position relative to the container view
    Point? relativeToContainerPosition = e.GetPosition((View)sender);
}
The argument to GetPosition() determines what the position is relative to.

Swipe Gestures
A swipe recogniser can detect the direction of a swipe.
&lt;BoxView Color="Red"
         HeightRequest="200"
         WidthRequest="200"&gt;

    &lt;BoxView.GestureRecognizers&gt;
        &lt;SwipeGestureRecognizer Swiped="OnSwipeGesture"
                                Direction="Left" /&gt;
    &lt;/BoxView.GestureRecognizers&gt;

&lt;/BoxView&gt;
The event handler can inspect the direction:
private void OnSwipeGesture(object sender, SwipedEventArgs e)
{
    lblMessage.Text = $"Swiped {e.Direction}";
}

Several Swipe Directions
We can configure multiple recognisers if we want to respond to different directions.
&lt;BoxView Color="Red"
         HeightRequest="200"
         WidthRequest="200"&gt;

    &lt;BoxView.GestureRecognizers&gt;
        &lt;SwipeGestureRecognizer Swiped="OnSwipeGesture"
                                Direction="Left" /&gt;

        &lt;SwipeGestureRecognizer Swiped="OnSwipeGesture"
                                Direction="Right" /&gt;

        &lt;SwipeGestureRecognizer Swiped="OnSwipeGesture"
                                Direction="Up" /&gt;

        &lt;SwipeGestureRecognizer Swiped="OnSwipeGesture"
                                Direction="Down" /&gt;
    &lt;/BoxView.GestureRecognizers&gt;

&lt;/BoxView&gt;
All four can use the same event handler.
private void OnSwipeGesture(object sender, SwipedEventArgs e)
{
    lblMessage.Text = $"Swiped {e.Direction}";
}
The event arguments tell us which direction was detected.
